>> delegation
   vouchryx issues delegation tokens and answers revocations on the compose
     network only, never published to the host (see the service's own comment
     in compose.yaml). The gateway polls its revocations at
     http://vouchryx:4310/v1/revocations and verifies a chain against the key set it polls
     there (or, with VOUCHRYX_SIGNER=file, the copy at /opt/agent-stack/delegation-public/vouchryx.jwks.json).
     vouchryx signs with the key keyryx certifies (VOUCHRYX_SIGNER); the root pin is
     kept in .env as VOUCHRYX_KEYRYX_ROOT_PIN, named here, never its value.
     POST /v1/revoke needs VOUCHRYX_REVOKE_KEYS from .env as a bearer key, and
     reaches vouchryx only from inside this compose network, the same way an
     operator revokes a policy plane key today: from a container on it, not the host.

Up, and every check passed.

  The gateway answers on this box only. Nothing here is reachable from another
  machine, which is the default on purpose. On the box itself:

      ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://<public-ip>:4100

  When you want agents elsewhere to call it, publish it deliberately. Editing
  .env is the way; re-running this script will not do it for you:

      cd /opt/agent-stack
      sed -i 's/^GATEWAY_BIND=.*/GATEWAY_BIND=<public-ip>/' .env
      docker compose up -d
      # then, from anywhere: ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://<public-ip>:4100

  Port 4100 is then open to the internet. Put a cloud security group in front
  of it: ufw will not do it, because Docker's own iptables rules bypass ufw's
  INPUT chain and a published container port stays reachable through a 'deny'.
  Client keys are ON, minted by this installer because it was given no keys file.
  Your agents now need a key: every call to the gateway carries

      x-fuse-key       the secret in /opt/agent-stack/agent-key (it is not printed here)
      x-fuse-agent-id  an id under agent://local/agents/, such as agent://local/agents/support-bot
      x-fuse-run-id    its run, as before

  A call with no key, or one the gateway does not hold, is refused 401, and the key
  speaking as an id outside agents/ is refused 403 identity_mismatch. To give each
  agent its own key and unit, bring a keys file and an identity map, both together
  (README, "Client keys and the identity map").
  Felyx, the console's copilot, calls this gateway too, with the key this
  installer minted for it (GENARYX_COPILOT_FUSE_KEY in .env, sent as x-fuse-key and
  not printed here), bound in the identity map to its own agent id and no other.
  The gateway holds no provider key: it forwards the caller's own key, as before, and
  says so at startup. Give ANTHROPIC_KEY_FILE to hold the key on the gateway (CLAUDE.md
  invariant 39).
  Agent tokens are ON (the default). The agent agent://local/agents/operator enrols at vouchryx, not published to the
  host, with the secret in /opt/agent-stack/agent-token.key (0600, never printed). It
  exchanges that for a five-minute token bound to its DPoP key. It asks for the
  audience with resource=https://stack-single.local (RFC 8707; audience= works too). A request that
  names none is refused audience_not_allowed. It then sends
  Authorization: DPoP <token> with a proof (carrying ath, RFC 9449). Stop the agent with one call:
  POST http://vouchryx:4310/v1/revoke with ban, using VOUCHRYX_REVOKE_KEYS from .env.
  The MCP broker's tool-call check fails closed (CLAUDE.md invariant 75): a tools/call the gateway's check does not answer,
  or that names no x-fuse-run-id, is refused. Each static key the broker admits is also a gateway key of the same id and
  secret, bound to the one agent it speaks as, and named on the gateway's static list below.
  Static keys still admitted at the gateway: felyx,broker,stack-single,drills-broker
  Static keys still admitted at the MCP broker: stack-single,drills-broker (stack-single is the installer's own check key there; felyx and broker at the gateway are the console and the broker's taint relay)
  static keys still admitted; move these agents to tokens, or name each one in STATIC_KEY_IDS on purpose.
  Operator-started profiles kept on this run. This installer never removes them; a running
  container of one is re-upped at this run's pinned image.
  none running on this box

  The tool hold is ON: an agent's tool call that nothing grants waits for a person, and model
  calls pass. Give an agent a tool: ./install.sh agent-allow <agent> <tool[,tool]|all> --why 'text'.

  Console sign-in, created on this run. The password is not printed here: it is in one
  file, mode 0600, that holds nothing else:

      user      ops
      file      /opt/agent-stack/console-password
      read it   sudo cat /opt/agent-stack/console-password    (delete the file once you have saved the password)

  The console is on loopback by design, so it is reached over a tunnel. This
  box runs the WireGuard side and issues your device its own peer config:
  sign in, open Remote, and take the QR. The config is shown once.

      console  https://console.genaryx.internal   (once your tunnel is up)
      tunnel   <public-ip>:51820/udp

  That name is private and its certificate is self-issued, so each device you
  use needs two one-time steps:

      echo "10.9.0.1 console.genaryx.internal" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
      # then trust this box's own CA:
      #   docker compose exec caddy cat /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt

  Both disappear once you set a real domain and CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN in .env:
  the certificate becomes publicly trusted and nothing needs installing.
  A passkey cannot be enrolled until one of these is done - WebAuthn refuses a
  bare IP and refuses an untrusted certificate, so http://10.9.0.1 is not a
  place the ceremony can run.

  Clearing a run's taint label (POST /v1/fuse/declassify on the gateway) needs
  its own key, GATEWAY_DECLASSIFY_KEY in /opt/agent-stack/.env, sent as the
  x-fuse-declassify-key header. It is not the console or admin key, and it is
  not printed here.

  The gateway clamps the budget a caller declares for one run (the
  x-fuse-budget-usd header, or a policy's default) to 5.00 USD,
  so an agent cannot give its own run a bigger one. A budget set in the Cloud
  is yours and is never clamped. To move it, set RUN_BUDGET_CEILING_USD in
  /opt/agent-stack/.env (a positive number of dollars, at most six decimals) and run
  docker compose up -d. It bounds one run, not an agent: a new run id starts a new one.

  The gateway's agent firewall runs in enforce: when a run has read untrusted
  input and the model then asks for an exec, write or network-egress action, the
  answer is withheld (HTTP 403, taint_blocked). To run it in shadow (record only)
  or off instead, set TOKENFUSE_FIREWALL to shadow or off in /opt/agent-stack/.env and
  run docker compose up -d tokenfuse-gateway. Running ./install.sh with the same
  variable checks the value before it writes it.

  A chain verifier (agent-conform) re-checks the hash chain of every event
  stream on this box every five minutes. A break becomes a high alert on the
  same bus your notifier already reads. It can write one file, its own, and
  nothing else on the bus.

  Killing a run, setting a budget, deciding an approval, and issuing or
  revoking a device all need a passkey: a road into the control plane is not
  something a stolen session should be able to mint quietly. That passkey can
  only be enrolled at https://console.genaryx.internal over the tunnel, so the sequence
  for your first session is:

      1. import /opt/agent-stack/console.genaryx.internal.conf (or scan the QR this run
         printed above) into your WireGuard client, and connect
      2. open https://console.genaryx.internal, trusting this box's own CA first if
         no CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN was set (see above)
      3. sign in with the password above
      4. enrol a passkey under Session > Passkeys

  Only after step 4 do kill, budget, approval and device commands work. A
  passkey cannot be enrolled over the SSH forward below: WebAuthn checks the
  origin, and that forward is http://localhost, never console.genaryx.internal.

  SSH stays as the way to read the console before the tunnel exists, not to
  act on it:

      ssh -L 17420:<public-ip>:7420 root@<public-ip>
      open http://localhost:17420

  Manage it:

      cd /opt/agent-stack && docker compose ps
      cd /opt/agent-stack && docker compose logs -f tokenfuse-gateway
      cd /opt/agent-stack && docker compose down     # stop, keeping every volume

  The error budget is measured every six hours (verdryx-slo; SLO_SIGNAL=0 turns
  it off), and the gateway's spend is exported for FinOps every 15 minutes, over
  both doors (focus-export), where the FinOps console reads it once you start it.
  Nothing on this box runs the other governance routines (crypto trend, quality
  drift, identity sweep) on a schedule. Run one by hand with docker compose exec,
  or see the README for how stack-up and stack-k8s do it.

exit=0
